  "live_records": {
    "q-73845": {
      "dom_id": "step-q73845",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2{z}^{2}$ at the point $(1,1,1,0).$",
      "worked_solution": "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2{z}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2{z}^{2})=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2{z}^{2})=-4z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(1,1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,1,1) & =2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,1,1) & =2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,1,1) & =-4\\cdot 1=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-0 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,1,1)+(y-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,1,1)+(z-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,1,1) \\\\ w & =2(x-1)+2(y-1)-4(z-1) \\\\ w & =2x-2+2y-2-4z+4 \\\\ -2x-2y+4z+w & =0 \\\\ 2x+2y-4z-w & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
