    "q-73869": {
      "dom_id": "step-q73869",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "At which points on the surface $z=x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector  $v\\,=\\,⟨-2,4,2⟩?$",
      "worked_solution": "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{y} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{y}+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨{e}^{y},\\,x{e}^{y}+2y,\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨-2,4,2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨{e}^{y},\\,x{e}^{y}+2y,\\,-1⟩=⟨-2,4,2⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}c{e}^{y}=-2 \\\\ cx{e}^{y}+2cy=4 \\\\ -c=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=-2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-2{e}^{y}=-2\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=-2$ and $y=0$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n-2x{e}^{0}+2(-2)(0)=4\\,⟹\\,x=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=-2$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & =(-2){e}^{0}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector $v$ at $(-2,0,-2).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
