    "q-129560": {
      "dom_id": "step-q129560",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "At which point on the surface $z=4-2{x}^{2}-5{y}^{2}$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector $v=⟨-2,-5,1⟩?$",
      "worked_solution": "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-2{x}^{2}-5{y}^{2})=-4x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-2{x}^{2}-5{y}^{2})=-10y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨-4x,\\,-10y,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨-2,-5,1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨-4x,\\,-10y,\\,-1⟩=⟨-2,-5,1⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-4cx=-2 \\\\ -10cy=-5 \\\\ -c=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=-1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-4(-1)x=-2\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=-1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n-10(-1)y=-5\\,⟹\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $y=-\\frac{1}{2}$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =4-2{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-5{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =4-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector $v$ at $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{9}{4}).$",
